    {
      "filename": "\"Skull\" (a companion piece to \"Cherub\") - geograph.org.uk - 1015330.jpg",
      "title": "File:\"Skull\" (a companion piece to \"Cherub\") - geograph.org.uk - 1015330.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/%22Skull%22_%28a_companion_piece_to_%22Cherub%22%29_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1015330.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Skull%22_(a_companion_piece_to_%22Cherub%22)_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1015330.jpg",
      "width": 640,
      "height": 480,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Lairich Rig",
      "description": "\"Skull\" (a companion piece to \"Cherub\"). A pair of bronze sculptures, powder-coated with gold, can be seen on opposite corners of the Tron Theatre. Together, they form a single work: Cherub/Skull. \"Cherub\" was installed first; of the two, it has the more prominent location, and can be seen here: 938201.\nThe other half of this work is \"Skull\"; it was installed in the winter of 1998, and is located in a much quieter street. (For a different view of the skull, see: 1055858.)\n\nBoth works are by Kenny Hunter and were cast by Powderhall Bronze (for more works by the same artist, see: 949393, 948674, 546448, 305846, and 1069028).",
      "dateCreated": "2005-11-11",
      "categories": [
        "CC-BY-SA-2.0",
